Commentary of al-Biqa'i (Nazm al-Durar) on surah 20, verse 52:

When Musa (peace be upon him) understood what he intended, that engaging in that matter, which has no benefit, would lead to refutation and prolonged argument, and since the Torah had not yet been revealed to him at that time (it was revealed only after the destruction of Pharaoh), he did not follow him in that. ﴿He said﴾, cutting him off from it: ﴿Its knowledge is with my Lord﴾, that is, the One who has been good to me by sending me and teaching me arguments.

Since it was the custom of created beings to record reports in books, and since He, exalted is He, had appointed from among His angels those who preserve that for His servants, He addressed him in terms of what they know of their own circumstances: ﴿In a Book﴾, that is, the Preserved Tablet. And since it might occur to the mind of someone who thinks that a book is only kept out of fear of forgetting something or of ignorance of how to reach it while knowing its specifics, he negated that by saying: ﴿My Lord does not err﴾, that is, the One who has nurtured me as you have known and saved me from all the destruction you intended for me, and He has not gone astray from any of its aspects, nor has He forgotten any aspect through which some flaw could enter; ﴿nor does He forget﴾, that is, no forgetting ever occurs from Him regarding anything at all, of His reports or of others. In this there is an indication of rebuking the Jews: if the establishment of prophethood depended on the prophet reporting about everything he is asked about, it would follow that they should have doubted the prophethood of their own prophet (peace be upon him), because he did not inform Pharaoh of what he asked him about concerning the matter of past generations;

Then he connected to this what came before it of rational proof for the oneness of the Creator (al-Sani') and His choice (ikhtiyar). He said: ﴿Who made for you﴾ O creatures, ﴿the earth﴾, that is, most of it, ﴿a cradle﴾, which you spread out and lay upon. He made some of it mountains on which one cannot settle, some of it soft, where feet sink in, and some of it hard, and so on, with the other varieties you observe in it. ﴿And threaded for you therein roads﴾, that is, He made easy paths which you travel in lands that are smooth and rugged, and He placed them among the mountains, the valleys and the sands. He prepared for you in it, from benefits, waters and pastures, what makes that easy. He also made in it what can never be traversed at all. The nature (tabi'ah) of the whole is one relation, so had the Agent not been one and free in choice, this variation and this wondrous order would not exist. ﴿And sent down from the sky water﴾ which you see as one in color and taste.

And since what arises from it is more indicative of greatness, clearer to the beholder, and more manifest to minds,

(p-297) the Prophet (ﷺ) was immersed in the seas of majesty (jalal). He summoned to mind that the One who commanded him with this speech is in truth the One who speaks it, passing away from his own self and from all existence. So he expressed this by turning away from the third person (ghaybah) to the first-person speaker whose command is obeyed, with its majesty, in His words: ﴿So We brought forth﴾, that is, by what We have of majesty, to which diverse things submit, ﴿by it pairs﴾ [i.e.] kinds resembling one another, in which there is nothing that is single without a like, ﴿of various plants﴾, that is, very diverse in colors, quantities, benefits, natures and tastes;

Commentary of al-Biqa'i (Nazm al-Durar) on surah 20, verse 73:

Then they gave the reason for their magnifying of God and their contempt for Pharaoh by saying: ﴿Indeed we have believed in our Lord﴾, that is, the One who has been good to us all our lives despite our wrongdoing through disbelief and other things, ﴿that He may forgive us﴾ [without any benefit accruing to Him through the act or any harm that He would suffer through its omission], ﴿our sins﴾, which we repaid with His kindness. Then, after the general, they singled out a specific matter, saying: ﴿and what you compelled us to﴾ [and they clarified this by their saying]: ﴿of magic﴾, so that we might oppose with it the miracle. For our disobedience to you in it was the more complete, since God is more worthy of being feared. It is related that the Copts among the magicians were only two, and the rest were from the Children of Israel, whom Pharaoh compelled to learn magic. It is also related that they saw Moses, peace be upon him, asleep with his staff guarding him, and they said to Pharaoh: "Indeed, when the magician sleeps, his magic becomes void, so this cannot be opposed," but he refused them and compelled them to the opposition.

[And since the implied sense was: "So our Lord is the People of Piety and the People of Forgiveness," they joined to it, calling to mind His perfection]: ﴿And God﴾, that is, the One who unites the attributes of perfection, ﴿is better﴾ in recompense than you regarding what you promised us, ﴿and more enduring﴾ in reward and punishment. The apparent meaning is that God Most High delivered them from Pharaoh, and His saying Most High supports this: ﴿You two and whoever follows you will be the victors﴾ [al-Qasas: 35]. This was stated by Abu Hayyan. [And there will come at the end of al-Hadid what is explicit regarding their salvation];
